WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.300
Bitcoin crossing back above $80,000 gives TFSA holders in crypto ETFs a fresh chance to rebalance position sizes before any momentum fade. This is Modern Investing Techniques, episode 149. Quick reminder, everything we discuss here is for education and entertainment. The practice investment of the day uses simulated trades with no real money. I'm not a licensed financial advisor and this isn't financial advice.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:52.460
Always do your own research before putting real money to work. The S&amp;P 500 closed at 7,653, down 3 tenths of a percent. The NASDAQ composite closed at 25,980, down 8 tenths of a percent. The TSX composite closed at 36,714, up 3 tenths of a percent.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:01:10.240
Terror threats continue to weigh on cross-border supply chains, while mining and industrial names gave the TSX some support. Both the Bank of Canada and the Fed remain on hold into September, with roughly 60% odds of a 25 basis point cut by year end.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:32.820
Earning season is largely behind us, so sector rotation and macro data now drive price action. About 14 days ago we picked AAPL on a product redesign catalyst that ultimately closed down 0.81%, and the lesson was that volume confirmation must proceed any catalyst entry when sector rotation is active.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:41.680
Portfolio allocation to gold and bitcoin can serve as a risk reduction overlay when bond yields are artificially suppressed by policy.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:57.160
Ray Dalio's framework treats 10-15% gold plus a smaller bitcoin sleeve as a hedge that historically lowers overall volatility while lifting long-term returns, especially when fiscal deficits keep pushing real yields higher.

00:01:57.160 --> 00:02:14.520
In today's environment of elevated long-term treasury yields and tariff driven uncertainty, the approach translates directly to a TFSA or RRSP by swapping a slice of fixed income exposure for a low-cost gold ETF and a bitcoin spot ETF.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:31.560
Implementation is straightforward, screened for the lowest MER gold and bitcoin ETFs on your platform, size the combined sleeve to 10-15% of the fixed income bucket, and rebalance annually or when the sleeve drifts more than 3 percentage points.

00:02:32.180 --> 00:02:42.780
The strategy has worked best during periods of rising debt to GDP and policy uncertainty. The main risk remains short-term draw downs if risk assets rally sharply.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:58.460
This is a simulated trade for educational purposes only. No real money is involved. This is not financial advice. Today's pick is GMIN.TO, G mining ventures on the TSX in the precious metal sector.

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:12.040
The strategy is an earning surprise entry after strong Q2 results and reaffirmed guidance. The catalyst was a Q2 earnings beat with reaffirmed full-year guidance that triggered a 13% single-day move.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:19.640
Price cleared the prior session high on volume well above the 20-day average, with nearest support at the pre-earnings close.

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:29.420
Gap fill risk remains if broader precious metals rotation stalls, so the stop sits at the session low for a maximum 4% loss on the position.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:46.560
The target is plus 3% to plus 6% on continued momentum into the next session. Confidence is medium because the earnings catalyst is confirmed and volume is supportive, yet sector rotation into precious metals remains unconfirmed by broader market breadth.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:59.140
This trade isolates an earning surprise setup in a sector with limited recent exposure, letting listeners observe how volume confirmation interacts with a fresh catalyst before committing to a multi-day hold.

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:04.400
It also demonstrates the fixed horizon exit rule in action on a same-day basis.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:13.120
Last week's hold was NFI.TO on a catalyst driven entry tied to a domestic demand recovery narrative for transit equipment.

00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:24.280
The actual hold ran six calendar days from Tuesday open to Monday close. Entry came at $24.08, exit at $22.29.

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:32.240
The position lost 7.43% or $74.34 on a $1,000 stake.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:46.660
The domestic demand thesis failed to overcome broader rotation pressure once volume confirmation lagged. The rule now requires volume above the 20-day average before entering any catalyst-driven name already in a sector rotation.

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:55.120
The show used to quote a cumulative alpha figure across roughly 45 trades. It is gone from the scoreboard and that is deliberate.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:07.140
A number that quietly vanishes is the oldest tell in performance reporting. That figure blended trades whose entry and exit prices could not be tied back to the actual sessions the trade was held.

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.020
An audit could not reproduce it so it was not the shows to claim.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:19.380
The exit rule was the deeper problem. A position used to be closed on whichever session the next pre-market run happened to price it.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:24.000
So a Monday pick was held about five sessions and a Wednesday pick about one.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:29.040
Per trade performance was measuring the day of the week as much as the quality of the idea.

00:05:29.740 --> 00:05:39.200
The hold is now a fixed, published number of sessions. Some older trades mashed no market prices at all and they include the best and the worst results on the books.

00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:45.280
They stay published as history, flagged, and they are never blended into what the show says on air.

00:05:46.140 --> 00:05:56.320
What replaced it? From August 18th, 2026, every pick is scored under one written rule book. Entry at the first session open on or after the pick.

00:05:56.860 --> 00:06:03.100
Exit at the stop or at the fixed horizon. One position, $1,000, no discretionary exits.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:14.440
The rules and the full trade by trade ledger, including the losers and the voided picks, are published for anyone to check at the Modern Investing Performance page at neranetwork.com.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:23.560
An invitation to check with no destination is not an invitation. The honest cost stated plainly. The record is now small.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:29.960
So for the next several weeks, the alpha number will be based on a handful of trades and will not mean much on its own.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:34.740
That is what an honest track record looks like early. Do not spin it.

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:43.840
This is exactly how a listener should audit any track record they are shown. Ask when the record started and whether that date was chosen after the fact.

00:06:44.560 --> 00:06:51.700
Ask what the exit rule is and whether it was fixed in advance. Ask whether losers and abandoned positions are included.

00:06:51.700 --> 00:06:59.980
Ask whether the individual trades are published or only the summary. A record that cannot answer those four questions is a story.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:12.620
Portfolio Performance, simulated at $1,000 per trade, shows 53 total trades at a 53% win rate and cumulative profit and loss of $202.74.

00:07:13.540 --> 00:07:24.260
Matched window alpha versus NASDAQ stands at negative 7.59% across two rules based trades, the headline number, with two few trades to call an edge.

00:07:24.740 --> 00:07:34.600
It is a scoreboard, not evidence. Average return per trade sits at plus 0.38%. Best trade plus 20.11%.

00:07:34.600 --> 00:07:39.820
Worst trade negative 11.80%. Current streak 3 losses.

00:07:40.520 --> 00:07:49.480
Imagine you own a Canadian bank ETF and see a headline that the 55 and older labor force participation rate has kept drifting lower since COVID.

00:07:50.100 --> 00:08:03.360
Your order fills at the previous close, but the real mechanism is that lower participation signals slower consumer spending and weaker loan demand, which can compress net interest margins faster than headline unemployment figures suggest.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:11.360
In the most recent data, the 55 plus cohort never recovered its pre-pandemic level and continues to trend down.

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:17.540
A dynamic that typically shows up in bank and consumer discretionary names within two to three sessions.

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:29.060
What most retail investors miss is that participation rates are released monthly with the jobs report and can be cross checked against the same day volume in rate sensitive ETFs.

00:08:29.060 --> 00:08:35.280
Professionals always look at that volume spike first before assuming the headline is already priced in.

00:08:36.100 --> 00:08:45.520
The concrete misconception to avoid is treating the unemployment rate as the only labor market signal. Participation trends often lead sector rotation by days.

